<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Details of Data Processing</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide additional details on data processing.</p>
</div>
<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Robust Multi-Template Complementation Mechanism</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. (2024c)</a></cite>, consider a knowledge fact <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> represented as a triple <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(subject,~relation,~object)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(subject,~relation,~object)</annotation></semantics></math>, such as <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Painblanc,~locatedin,~France)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Painblanc,~locatedin,~France)</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a sentence <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\text{map}(subject,~relation)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>map</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\text{map}(subject,~relation)</annotation></semantics></math> that maps the subject and relation (e.g., <span id="A6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Painblanc is located in</span>), an LLM <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is considered to have memorized <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> if it can predict <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=\text{map}(object)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>map</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\text{map}(object)</annotation></semantics></math> by mapping the object (e.g., <span id="A6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">France</span>) such that <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\subseteq\mathcal{M}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\subseteq\mathcal{M}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is a probabilistic model influenced by different mapping templates and sampling probabilities, we design <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{map}}=21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>map</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{map}}=21</annotation></semantics></math> different mappings for each knowledge fact <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. With the temperature set to 0.7, the model generates <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{sample}}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>sample</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{sample}}=10</annotation></semantics></math> outputs for each mapping. The degree to which the LLM memorizes <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is then calculated as:</p>
<table id="A6.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A6.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{k}^{\mathcal{M}}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{map}}}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{\text{sample}}}\mathbb{I}(y_{i}\subseteq\mathcal{M}^{j}(x_{i}))}{N_{\text{map}}\times N_{\text{sample}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>map</mtext></msub></msubsup><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>sample</mtext></msub></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>map</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>sample</mtext></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{k}^{\mathcal{M}}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{map}}}\sum_{j=1}^{N_{\text{sample}}}\mathbb{I}(y_{i}\subseteq\mathcal{M}^{j}(x_{i}))}{N_{\text{map}}\times N_{\text{sample}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the results from the <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th mapping, <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th sampled output, and <math id="A6.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This approach effectively utilizes the characteristics of LLMs to evaluate their mastery of different data. However, as entities often have multiple aliases (e.g., <span id="A6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">USA</span> and <span id="A6.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">United States</span>), the singular entity labeling in the original dataset may introduce biases. To enhance robustness, a synonym mapping table (Table <a href="#A6.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ F.1 Robust Multi-Template Complementation Mechanism ‣ Appendix F Details of Data Processing ‣ Analyzing the Effects of Supervised Fine-Tuning on Model Knowledgefrom Token and Parameter Levels" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) is constructed to expand the set of equivalent entity names, significantly improving result accuracy.
This table is also used in judging the accuracy of LLMs’ answers after SFT.</p>
</div>
